# Dependency Convergence — Lutece 8

## The Baseline

Lutece 8 targets **Jakarta EE 10** (`jakarta.platform:jakarta.jakartaee-api:10.0.0`, `provided`, declared in the parent). Every dependency must stay inside that generation.

| Spec | EE 10 — correct | EE 11 — do NOT introduce |
|------|-----------------|--------------------------|
| Servlet | 6.0 | 6.1 |
| CDI | 4.0.x | 4.1 |
| `jakarta.annotation-api` | 2.1.x | 3.0.0 |
| `jakarta.el-api` | 5.0.x | 6.x |
| `jakarta.validation-api` | 3.0.x | 3.1.x |
| `weld-junit5` | 4.0.5.Final (Weld 5.1.6) | 5.x (Weld 6) |

Pulling an EE 11 artifact in, usually by bumping a test library, silently drags the whole test classpath forward and breaks at runtime, not at compile time.

## Parent POM Version

Use the **latest released `lutece-global-pom` 8.x**. Read it from the release repository, never from memory:

```
https://dev.lutece.paris.fr/maven_repository/fr/paris/lutece/tools/lutece-global-pom/maven-metadata.xml
```

`scan-project.sh` writes it to `project.latestParent`. Always a released version, never a SNAPSHOT parent.

## What Each Parent Manages

Declare these with coordinates and scope only. A local `<version>` re-creates the divergence the parent exists to prevent.

| Managed for tests | 8.0.0 / 8.0.1 | 8.0.2 and later |
|---|---|---|
| `library-lutece-unit-testing` | 5.0.1 | 5.0.2 |
| `hibernate-validator` | 8.0.1.Final | 8.0.5.Final |
| `jaxb-runtime` | 4.0.5 | 4.0.9 |
| EL implementation | `org.glassfish:jakarta.el` 5.0.0-M1 | `org.glassfish.expressly:expressly` 5.0.0 |
| `jboss-logging` | not managed | 3.6.3.Final |
| `jakarta.el-api` | not managed | 5.0.1 |
| `jakarta.annotation-api` | not managed | 2.1.1 |

The EL implementation is the trap: each parent manages exactly one of the two artifacts. `org.glassfish:jakarta.el` never released past `5.0.0-M1` and was renamed `org.glassfish.expressly:expressly`, which is also what `hibernate-validator` depends on today. Declaring the artifact the parent does not manage yields a dependency without version and the build fails.

## Enforcer, from 8.0.2

Parents 8.0.0 and 8.0.1 only enforce the Maven and Java versions. From 8.0.2 a second execution runs on every scope except `provided`, test included:

- `requireUpperBoundDeps` **fails the build** when the resolved version of a transitive dependency is lower than what another dependency requires. `-Denforcer.dependencyRules.fail=false` turns it into a report, for diagnosis only.
- `banDuplicatePomDependencyVersions` fails on the same `groupId:artifactId` declared twice in one POM.
- `dependencyConvergence` **reports only**, listing every divergence including the harmless ones.

## Reading an Enforcer Failure

```
Rule 0: ...RequireUpperBoundDeps failed with message:
Require upper bound dependencies error for org.jboss.logging:jboss-logging:3.4.3.Final
paths to dependency are:
  +-my-plugin:1.0.0-SNAPSHOT
    +-org.hibernate.validator:hibernate-validator:8.0.1.Final
      +-org.jboss.logging:jboss-logging:3.4.3.Final
```

Maven resolves the **nearest** declaration, not the highest. When two paths disagree, the shallower one wins and can downgrade a transitive dependency below what another library requires.

Fix it in this order:

1. **Align the real versions**: bump the library that pulls the old artifact. No override to maintain.
2. **Pin in the parent's `dependencyManagement`** if several plugins hit the same conflict. Inherited by the whole graph.
3. **`<exclusions>`** only to remove an artifact that must genuinely disappear. An exclusion does not resolve a version conflict: with several paths to the artifact, excluding one leaves the divergence; excluding all removes the class and produces a `NoClassDefFoundError` at runtime.

Do NOT widen `excludedScopes` to silence a failure. That is the check, not the noise.

## Diagnosing

```bash
mvn dependency:tree -Dverbose -Dincludes=<groupId>:<artifactId>
mvn enforcer:enforce
```

`-Dverbose` shows the omitted-for-conflict branches, which is what identifies the winning path.

## Logging Backend

`jboss-logging` (used by Weld, SmallRye Config, Hibernate Validator) is a facade that picks its backend at first class load, in this order:

**JBoss LogManager → Log4j2 → SLF4J → Log4j 1.x → JDK**

On a Lutece classpath it selects `Log4j2LoggerProvider` natively, no bridge needed. Two consequences:

- If a dependency ever introduces `jboss-logmanager`, it takes priority over Log4j2 and silently diverts Weld / SmallRye / Hibernate Validator logs away from `log4j2.xml`. The enforcer will not catch it: a class arriving is not a version conflict. Force the backend with `-Dorg.jboss.logging.provider=log4j2` if needed.
- Those libraries log under `org.jboss.weld` / `io.smallrye.*`. With no explicit logger they inherit `<Root>`, which the test configuration sets to `ERROR`, so INFO and DEBUG are dropped. Add an explicit logger, or run with `LOG4J_LEVEL=debug`, when debugging CDI or config startup.
